# apn-autoconfig-proto-atdial transport quirk table v1
#
# Divergence in how a modem moves *data*, for devices where the standard 3GPP
# dial completes and the link still carries nothing. This is a different
# question from the capability table in apn-autoconfig-modem, which answers what
# a modem can be asked to do; that one stays keyed by AT+CGMI/AT+CGMM and stays
# empty until someone has watched a capability work.
#
# Format, tab-separated:
#
#   <vendor_id>	<product_id>	<model>	<key>	<value>
#
# `vendor_id` and `product_id` are the USB identifiers as they appear in sysfs,
# lowercase hex without the 0x. `product_id` and `model` may be `*`; `vendor_id`
# may not, because a rule that applies to every device is not a quirk. `model`
# matches AT+CGMM exactly and exists so an entry can be narrowed later without a
# format change. The most specific match wins: exact model, then product, then
# vendor.
#
# **Why this table is keyed by USB identifier and not by model.** Keying by what
# the modem reports about itself is the better shape, and it is what
# apn-autoconfig-modem does. It is not available here. These entries come from
# another project's hardware reports, and what those reports contain is the USB
# vendor — their production code branches on `idVendor == 8087` and nothing
# else. The AT+CGMM strings of these devices have not been observed by anyone
# whose notes this project has read, so putting a guessed model string here
# would be exactly the invention the "absent means untested" rule exists to
# prevent. The `model` column is present so that a measured string can narrow an
# entry the day someone reads one off a real device.
#
# Every entry names the evidence behind it. `third-party` means it comes from a
# report this project has read but not reproduced; nothing here has run on this
# project's own bench, and the runtime reports these paths as
# alpha/synthetic accordingly.
#
# Keys:
#
#   data_channel  `xmm`  — bind the first NCM channel to the context and start
#                          data after an address exists. Without it the address
#                          is assigned and no frames move.
#   dns_source    `xdns` — enable the vendor DNS report before activation and
#                          read servers from it afterwards. On these devices
#                          AT+CGCONTRDP returns nothing.
#   link_arp      `off`  — disable ARP on the link and route on-link with no
#                          gateway. The NCM channel does not answer ARP, so
#                          every packet otherwise dies in neighbour resolution.
#
# Intel XMM, as used by Fibocom L850 and L860. Evidence: luci-app-5gmodem
# 2.4.10, which folded these three behaviours into its own handler after live
# reports from an L850 and an L860 on one router, and whose notes record that
# both modules enumerate as 8087:095a — which is also why the model column
# cannot distinguish them here and is left open.
8087	095a	*	data_channel	xmm	# third-party
8087	095a	*	dns_source	xdns	# third-party
8087	095a	*	link_arp	off	# third-party
#
# A second Intel product id carrying an L860 profile in the same upstream tree.
# Same three behaviours, same evidence class, one step weaker: it is inferred
# from that project shipping an L860 metrics profile for this id, not from a
# report naming it while dialing.
8087	07f9	*	data_channel	xmm	# third-party
8087	07f9	*	dns_source	xdns	# third-party
8087	07f9	*	link_arp	off	# third-party
